---
import '../styles/portfolio-review.css';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Deep dive: Overdubbing - Design Engineer, Sage Baggott</title>
  </head>
  <body>

    <header class="masthead">
      <div class="wrap masthead__inner">
        <span class="masthead__title">Design Engineer - Sage Baggott</span>
        <nav class="nav">
          <a href="/portfolio">Work</a>
          <a href="/overdub" aria-current="page">Deep dive - Overdubbing</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main class="wrap">

      <div class="page-head" id="step-0"> <!-- Not really step 0, but putting the anchor here scrolls the page to the proper spot -->
        <p class="eyebrow">Boombox mobile &middot; Deep dive</p>
        <h1>Overdubbing</h1>

        <div class="page-head__body">
          <div class="frame page-head__media">
            <a class="shot" href="#lb-overdub-gif">
            <img src="/video/portfolio-review/overdubbing-workflow.gif" alt="Animated walkthrough of the overdub workflow, from setting the start point through saving the mix" width="558" height="558">
            </a>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="lede">For the last hackathon I participated in at Boombox, I had the idea to add on to our users'
              ability to make written and audio comments.
              Overdub lets a musician record a new part straight onto an existing track from their phone, then hear the two
              together before committing to anything.
            </p>
            <p class="lede">
              I knew this would be a really useful feature for musicians, audio engineers, and music producers to quickly
              get new ideas down that they could share with collaborators.
              I hacked together a POC in two days, demo'ed it, and was immediately asked, "When can we see this in the
              mobile app?". So I got to work recreating it in a way that could be used in production.</p>
            <p class="lede">
              Most of the hard parts were not visual. Microphone bleed, audio routing, round trip latency, animating
              waveforms and audio levels, and optimizing for low re-renders were all issues that had to be dealt with.
              These are the six states in order, with the thinking behind each.</p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="steps">

        <article class="step" id="step-1">
          <a class="shot" href="#lb-overdub-02"><img src="/images/portfolio-review/Boombox-overdub-02.png" alt="Overdub track screen with the playhead at zero" width="1206" height="2622"></a>
          <span class="step__num">Step 01</span>
          <h3>Set the starting point</h3>
          <p>The screen opens on the whole track with the overdub marker at zero. Tapping anywhere on the waveform moves that marker,
            so someone adding a part to the second chorus does not have to sit through the first two minutes to get there.
            The marker is a different color from playback position and carries its own timecode label, which means you can confirm the edit
            by looking at the waveform instead of reading the fine print underneath. The line below the waveform describes what is about to
            happen in a sentence rather than labeling a control. Light grey text indicates the inputs and outputs for recording.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="step" id="step-2">
          <a class="shot" href="#lb-overdub-01"><img src="/images/portfolio-review/Boombox-overdub-01.png" alt="Overdub track screen playing through the earpiece receiver" width="1206" height="2622"></a>
          <span class="step__num">Step 02</span>
          <h3>Routing around feedback</h3>
          <p>Recording with the speaker on means the microphone picks up the backing track and prints it into the take. When no headphones are connected,
            playback falls back to the earpiece receiver, and when placed near the ear, most of the bleed is avoided. The screen names the output that is live,
            explains why it was chosen, and says what to physically do about it. All three lines are amber rather than red, because this is a workable state and not an error.
            The screen also confirms the start point one more time, since the earlier tap happened on a different screen state.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="step" id="step-3">
          <a class="shot" href="#lb-overdub-03"><img src="/images/portfolio-review/Boombox-overdub-03.png" alt="Overdub recording in progress with level meter" width="1206" height="2622"></a>
          <span class="step__num">Step 03</span>
          <h3>Recording</h3>
          <p>During a take the interface focuses on recording. Record becomes Stop in the same position, the transport counts up against the fixed start marker,
            and an SVG level meter sits directly above the button so the performer can see they are actually being recorded. The route line turns red to make it unmistakable
            that the microphone is recording.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="step" id="step-4">
          <a class="shot" href="#lb-overdub-04"><img src="/images/portfolio-review/Boombox-overdub-04.png" alt="Review your recording, two track mix with latency control" width="1206" height="2622"></a>
          <span class="step__num">Step 04</span>
          <h3>Review the mix</h3>
          <p>The recording ends with a two track mix: the backing on top, the new recording below, each with its own volume fader and progress bar.
            The two are labeled by who made them, so they are never confused at a glance. The re-mixing happens entirely on the phone using TypeScript
            modules that tie the Expo library together with native code. Re-recording is a normal thing to do here, but it still throws away a take,
            so making the "Cancel & re-record" button quieter than the "Save" button ensures a deliberate press rather than the obvious next tap.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="step" id="step-5">
          <a class="shot" href="#lb-overdub-05"><img src="/images/portfolio-review/Boombox-overdub-05.png" alt="Review screen re-mixing after a latency change" width="1206" height="2622"></a>
          <span class="step__num">Step 05</span>
          <h3>Latency adjustment</h3>
          <p>Every device puts a few milliseconds between hearing the backing track and capturing the voice, and the amount shifts with the headphones and the phone.
            The latency slider moves the recorded track against the backing so the performer can nudge it until it sits in the pocket. Each change quickly rebuilds the mix,
            which is done with Expo modules which connect the Expo library with native Swift or Kotlin code.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="step" id="step-6">
          <a class="shot" href="#lb-overdub-06"><img src="/images/portfolio-review/Boombox-overdub-06.png" alt="Save your recording sheet" width="1206" height="2622"></a>
          <span class="step__num">Step 06</span>
          <h3>Save and share</h3>
          <p>The save sheet asks for two names because it produces two files. The raw recording stays useful for real production work, and the mix is a preview.
            Saying that plainly under the toggle keeps the mix from being mistaken for a master later on. Both name fields pre-fill from the source track so nothing has to be typed,
            and "Save & share" is the primary action, since the most common use case is to send the mix to somebody.</p>
        </article>

      </div>

      <section class="retro">
        <div class="retro__col">
          <h3>Some things that went well</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>I came up with a hackathon project that was very well received and turned it into a production feature in a short amount of time.</li>
            <li>The overall functionality works very well, with the mixing operations happening quickly on-device.</li>
            <li>Considering the number of actions and animations happening at once, the layout is clean and pretty easy to scan.</li>
            <li>The UI is snappy and the re-renders are kept to a minimum due to many improvements to the code logic.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="retro__col">
          <h3>Some things I would change</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>The playhead isn't draggable, you have to tap on the waveform. It's also difficult to tap at the very beginning, which is why I have the "Overdub from 00:00" link.</li>
            <li>The affordance of the latency adjustment slider is a bit off. It's hard to see when your finger is dragging, and the increments need to be smaller values.</li>
            <li>Long titles are truncated and hard to tell apart if similar. I would find ways to minimize that, either with smaller text or different placement.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="foot">
        <span>Design Engineer - Sage Baggott</span>
        <span><a href="/portfolio">&larr; Back to work</a></span>
      </div>

    </main>

